{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PCxo17c2YtH8zvk1nttCzS","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# WI-226 — Dispatcher decomposition: characterize, then separate decisions from effects (repo-review-2026-07-18 H-02, slice B)\n2\t\n3\t**Branch:** dualplan-routing-fix · **Filed:** 2026-07-18 (review H-02\n4\t\"required next move\", main slice; absorbs L-04 as its trailing test-split) ·\n5\t**BuildTier:** strong · **SafetyClass:** high-risk (the integrator/publish\n6\tstate machines — single-WI traincar).\n7\t\n8\t## Why\n9\t\n10\t`agent_dispatch.py` is 2,292 lines with `dispatch_run` at 698 lines /\n11\tcomplexity 84, and `integrate_train`, `publish_integration`,\n12\t`pack_traincars`, `_salvage_round_evidence` all over-limit. WI-218 moved code\n13\tverbatim behind seams — it deliberately did not decompose. The review's\n14\tverdict stands: the code is safe to change because the tests are excellent,\n15\tnot because the control flow is legible. Correctness hardening keeps landing\n16\there (WI-220/222/223/224), so illegibility compounds where the risk is\n17\thighest.\n18\t\n19\t## Scope (staged; each stage lands green independently)\n20\t\n21\t1. **Characterize.** A design doc (`docs/specs/` companion or §-section\n22\t   here) enumerating `dispatch_run`'s states, transitions, and effect points\n23\t   (git refs, worktrees, sessions, journal, run-state) — written from the\n24\t   code + tests, reviewed for honesty against both. No code change.\n25\t2. **Extract pure decisions.** Move decision logic (what to do next, how to\n26\t   rank, when to stop) into side-effect-free functions with table-driven\n27\t   tests; effects (git/session/filesystem) stay behind the existing seams.\n28\t   Measurable targets set per boundary at stage start — e.g. `dispatch_run`\n29\t   complexity 84 → a stated target, not \"smaller\" — with the WI-225 ratchet\n30\t   re-stamped *downward* each stage.\n31\t3. **Test split (the L-04 fold-in).** Re-home tests along the new behavior\n32\t   boundaries only after stages 1–2 stabilize them; no split before then.\n33\t\n34\tBehavior-preserving throughout: public surface, exit codes, journal events,\n35\tand ref discipline unchanged; the full-suite + G3 bar after every stage.\n36\t\n37\t**Non-goals:** no reconcile-policy or spec-§9 semantic changes; no\n38\tarbitrary file-count targets; no downstream migration burden (seams and\n39\tpublic names stay).\n40\t\n41\t## Done-when\n42\t\n43\tEach stage's target met and recorded in the log with before/after complexity\n44\tnumbers; WI-225's baseline strictly smaller than at start; full suite + G3\n45\tgate green at every stage close.\n46\t\n47\t## Stage 1 characterization (2026-07-18)\n48\t\n49\tThis is the behavior model for the extraction. It was read from\n50\t`agent_dispatch.dispatch_run` and checked against the dispatcher, train,\n51\tintegration, recovery, migration, review, and dual-plan tests. The model is\n52\tdescriptive: it does not add a state or change a policy.\n53\t\n54\t### State carried by the loop\n55\t\n56\tThe coordinator state is the tuple below. A transition may update more than one\n57\tcomponent, but the durable Git evidence remains authoritative after a crash.\n58\t\n59\t| Component | Values / meaning |\n60\t|---|---|\n61\t| integration relation | absent cold start; equal; development strictly ahead; integration strictly ahead; diverged |\n62\t| active lanes | train ID to worker process, reserved WIs, base SHA, worktree, spine flag, open log handle |\n63\t| parked lanes | `resume`, `waiting`, `ready-to-integrate`, `blocked`, `train-end`, `integrated`, `blocked-done`, `needs-human`, `needs-re-review`, `rework`, `dual-paged`, or `quarantined` |\n64\t| excluded WIs | unreadable or unprovable reservation evidence, held out of the schedulable frontier for this run |\n65\t| controls | pause reason, blackout window, gate policy, worker ceiling, and pending human ask |\n66\t| frontier | registry rows at the integration ref, evaluated against reservations and exclusions, then packed into traincars |\n67\t\n68\t### Phases and transitions\n69\t\n70\t1. **Launch / preflight.** Parse jobs and the dual-prompt map, validate the\n71\t   agent command, acquire the single-coordinator lock, read declared policies,\n72\t   require a development branch, apply the migration ceiling, and reconcile the\n73\t   legacy compatibility window. Any parse, preflight, lock, or detached-HEAD\n74\t   failure exits `EXIT_PREFLIGHT` without dispatching.\n75\t2. **Integration-ref reconciliation.** A cold start creates the integration\n76\t   ref. Equal heads require no ref change. Development strictly ahead advances\n77\t   the integration ref by CAS. Integration strictly ahead resumes publication.\n78\t   Divergence records both heads and exits `EXIT_NEEDS_HUMAN`; neither history\n79\t   is rewritten.\n80\t3. **Durable-train reconstruction.** Reservation refs are grouped by train.\n81\t   Unreadable reservations are excluded. A train already marked done at the\n82\t   integration ref releases stale reservations and becomes `integrated`.\n83\t   Missing train branches or foreign WI claims quarantine only the affected\n84\t   ownership set. Otherwise branch evidence classifies the train as `blocked`,\n85\t   `ready-to-integrate`, or `resume`.\n86\t4. **Dynamic refill loop.** Every pass recomputes pause/blackout controls and\n87\t   the frontier from the integration ref. When dispatch is allowed, due\n88\t   `resume`/`waiting` lanes start before new reservati
